WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
spaCy.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.184
In the LLM era, why still use classical NLP?

3
00:00:05.184 --> 00:00:05.930
Economics.

4
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:06.900
Let's see it.

5
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.133
spaCy is industrial-strength classical NLP.

6
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:15.355
It's a fast, Cython-powered pipeline with opinionated defaults.

7
00:00:15.355 --> 00:00:18.467
You load a pretrained pipeline, and it just works.

8
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.633
Call nlp on a text, and the whole pipeline runs in one pass.

9
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.033
It tokenizes.

10
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:26.633
Tags parts of speech.

11
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:28.367
Parses dependencies.

12
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:29.700
Lemmatizes.

13
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.791
And finds named entities.

14
00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:34.800
Every stage annotates the same Doc object.

15
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Here's entity extraction.

16
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.700
We load the small English pipeline.

17
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.933
One call processes the sentence.

18
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.101
Then we group each entity under its label.

19
00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:50.700
NASA, SpaceX and the FAA are organizations, and September twenty eighth is a date.

20
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.200
So when do you pick which?

21
00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:00.733
Extracting entities from a million documents costs pennies on CPU with spaCy, versus real money with an LLM.

22
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.866
spaCy is fast and local.

23
00:01:02.866 --> 00:01:05.767
An LLM brings latency and rate limits.

24
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:11.667
Use spaCy for shallow, well-defined extraction, and save the LLM for what needs reasoning.

25
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:14.767
Here's the economics in practice.

26
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:21.033
Retrieval improves sharply when you can filter by metadata, like only chunks mentioning JPL.

27
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:27.267
Running spaCy over forty thousand chunks takes about twenty minutes on one CPU and costs nothing.

28
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:32.700
The same job through an LLM is tens of dollars and hours of rate-limited calls.

29
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:34.788
Let's recap.

30
00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:37.600
spaCy is a fast, opinionated pipeline.

31
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.800
One call gives tokens, tags, parses, lemmas, and entities.

32
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.133
It wins on cost and speed for shallow extraction.

33
00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:48.551
Use the LLM only where reasoning is needed.

34
00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:51.233
Next up: Hugging Face library basics.
